## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical concurrency on bare-metal MCUs: interrupt-safe data sharing, wrap-around handling, and overflow behavior in a tight, deterministic path.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffers with power-of-two sizing
- Volatile indices and memory visibility between ISR and main code
- Minimal interrupt masking to avoid latency spikes
- Edge-case testing for wrap and overflow

## Code Example
```c
#define N 32
#define MASK (N-1)
static uint8_t buf[N];
static volatile uint8_t head = 0; // consumer index
static volatile uint8_t tail = 0; // producer index

int enqueue_byte(uint8_t b) {
  uint8_t next = (tail + 1) & MASK;
  if (next == head) return -1; // full
  buf[tail] = b;
  // memory barrier if available
  tail = next;
  return 0;
}

int dequeue_byte(uint8_t *out) {
  if (head == tail) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[head];
  head = (head + 1) & MASK;
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-producer/multi-consumer safely?
- What tests would you run on a simulated interrupt workload to prove correctness?

## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this design for power-failure resume without data loss? 
- How to choose N and payload size for latency/throughput trade-offs in a busy IPC path?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a simple but non-trivial producer-consumer synchronization at the memory-ordering level, with attention to cache effects and correctness under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded circular buffer
- Lightweight per-slot synchronization flags
- Memory ordering: release/acquire
- False sharing and cache-line padding

## Code Example
